 ## Overview
-NeRF Importance Sampling is a two-stage volume rendering strategy that refines the discrete sampling distribution along a ray by drawing additional points from a probability density function (PDF) derived from the coarse stage's opacity weights [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of insufficient sampling in high-opacity regions, which leads to aliasing and noise, by concentrating fine samples where the volume contributes most to the final color [S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from its siblings, such as [NerfAcc Rendering Pipeline](1619-nerfacc-rendering-pipeline.md) which uses learned acceleration structures, and [VF-NeRF](3138-vf-nerf.md) which uses view-dependent feature grids, by relying on a simple, differentiable stratified resampling of the coarse alpha weights without auxiliary networks or complex data structures [S1, unofficial].
+NeRF Importance Sampling is a two-stage volume rendering strategy that refines the distribution of sample points along a ray by drawing additional samples from a probability distribution defined by the opacity weights of a coarse neural network pass [S1, S2, unofficial]. It solves the inefficiency of uniform sampling by concentrating computational effort on regions with high expected density or opacity, thereby improving rendering quality with minimal overhead [S1, S2, unofficial]. It differs from its siblings by using a specific stratified sampling procedure on the cumulative distribution function (CDF) of coarse alpha weights to generate fine samples, whereas [NerfAcc Rendering Pipeline](1619-nerfacc-rendering-pipeline.md) uses learned acceleration structures, [VF-NeRF](3138-vf-nerf.md) uses a voxel-based hierarchical representation, and [Voxel-based Volume Rendering](3845-voxel-based-volume-rendering.md) relies on spatial hashing for density queries [S1, S2, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-The method operates in two stages: a coarse stage that samples uniformly and computes opacity weights, and a fine stage that resamples based on those weights [S1, unofficial].
